eplacement-bijection conjecture for equivalent binary tree patterns\n\nFrom papers\n\nLet $s$s and $t$t be equivalent binary tree patterns, meaning that the binary trees avoiding $s$s and those avoiding $t$t are equinumerous. A top-down replacement, bottom-up replacement, and left--right reflection are the pattern transformations described in the paper, each inducing the corresponding transformation on binary trees.\n\nReplacement-bijection conjecture. If $s$s and $t$t are equivalent, then there is a sequence of top-down replacements, bottom-up replacements, and left--right reflections that produces a bijection from binary trees avoiding $s$s to binary trees avoiding $t$t.\n\nThe conjecture proposes that these explicit transformations generate all equivalences between binary tree pattern-avoidance classes. The paper states that the assertion holds computationally for patterns with at most $7$7 leaves, while the general case remains open.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nSources & references View supporting material\n\n## Primary source\n\nEric S. Rowland, “Pattern avoidance in binary trees”, arXiv:0809.0488 (2010).\n\nbijectionsbinary-treespattern-avoidance\n\n0\n\n1 week ago\n\n## Solutions 0\n\nSign in to submit a solution.\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConjectures | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/conjectures)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Discrete structures Conjecture refuted First preprint 2026 A disconnected counterexample to the token-radius conjecture The graph consisting of one edge and two isolated vertices has Laplacian spectral radius 2, as does its two-token graph, but it is not a star.Refuted ... Here the token vertices are k-subsets, adjacency means that the